= a
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}
{c}

After division by $x^2$, the <linear ordinary differential equation> becomes
$$
y'-\frac{y}{x^2}=\frac{e^{-1/x}}{x^2}.
$$
The <integrating factor> is $e^{1/x}$. Thus
$$
\frac{d}{dx}\left(e^{1/x}y\right)=\frac1{x^2},\qquad e^{1/x}y=-\frac1x+C.
$$
The <initial condition> $y(1)=1$ gives $C=e+1$, so
$$
\boxed{y(x)=\left(e+1-\frac1x\right)e^{-1/x},\qquad x>0.}
$$

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}
{c}

For $y=x$, the <derivatives> are $y'=1$ and $y''=0$, and substitution gives $2(x-1)-2(x-1)=0$.

Use <reduction of order>: set $y=xv$, so $y'=v+xv'$ and $y''=2v'+xv''$. Substitution and cancellation give
$$
x^2v''+2x^2v'=0.
$$
Since $x>0$, this reduces to $v''+2v'=0$, hence $v'=Ae^{-2x}$ and $v=C_1+C_2e^{-2x}$. The <general solution> is
$$
\boxed{y(x)=C_1x+C_2xe^{-2x},\qquad x>0.}
$$
The two solutions are <linearly independent>, since their ratio $e^{-2x}$ is nonconstant; equivalently, their <Wronskian> is $-2x^2e^{-2x}\ne0$.

= c
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}
{c}

The <homogeneous linear differential equation> $y''+y=0$ has <general solution> $C_1\cos x+C_2\sin x$. For a <particular solution>, use <variation of parameters> with $y_1=\cos x$, $y_2=\sin x$ and <Wronskian> one:
$$
y_p=-\cos x\int x\sin^2x\,dx+\sin x\int x\sin x\cos x\,dx.
$$
The <double-angle formulas> and <integration by parts> give
$$
\int x\sin^2x\,dx=\frac{x^2}{4}-\frac{x\sin2x}{4}-\frac{\cos2x}{8},\qquad
\int x\sin x\cos x\,dx=-\frac{x\cos2x}{4}+\frac{\sin2x}{8}.
$$
Substitution simplifies to $-x^2\cos x/4+x\sin x/4+\cos x/8$. Absorb the last term into the <homogeneous linear differential equation> solution. Therefore
$$
\boxed{y(x)=C_1\cos x+C_2\sin x+\frac{x\sin x-x^2\cos x}{4}.}
$$
Indeed, applying $d^2/dx^2+1$ to $x\sin x$ gives $2\cos x$, and applying it to $x^2\cos x$ gives $2\cos x-4x\sin x$; their difference divided by four is the required $x\sin x$.

