In <Cartesian coordinates>, a <parametrization> of the <curve> is
$$
x(\theta)=f(\theta)\cos\theta,\qquad y(\theta)=f(\theta)\sin\theta.
$$
Differentiate using the <product rule>:
$$
x'=f'\cos\theta-f\sin\theta,\qquad y'=f'\sin\theta+f\cos\theta.
$$
Squaring and adding cancels the mixed terms, giving $(x')^2+(y')^2=(f')^2+f^2$. The <arc length> is the integral of the speed, so
$$
\boxed{L=\int_0^{2\pi}\sqrt{f(\theta)^2+f'(\theta)^2}\,d\theta.}
$$
This assumes the <parametrization> is piecewise continuously differentiable and counts length with multiplicity if the <curve> is retraced.

= b
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}
{c}

The <cardioid> has <reflection symmetry> about the $x$ axis because $1+\cos(-\theta)=1+\cos\theta$. It passes through $(2,0)$ at $\theta=0$, through $(0,\pm1)$ at $\theta=\pm\pi/2$, and has a <cusp> at the origin when $\theta=\pi$. These points and the following sketch show its orientation.

\Image[mathematics-iii-cardioid.png]
{title=The cardioid and unit circle, with the three regions between them shaded}

Using the <polar coordinates> formula for <arc length>,
$$
L=\int_0^{2\pi}\sqrt{(1+\cos\theta)^2+\sin^2\theta}\,d\theta
=\int_0^{2\pi}2\left|\cos\frac\theta2\right|\,d\theta.
$$
The <absolute value> changes sign at $\theta=\pi$, so
$$
\boxed{L=4\int_0^\pi\cos\frac\theta2\,d\theta=8.}
$$

= c
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}
{c}

The <cardioid> meets the <unit circle> where $1+\cos\theta=1$, namely at $\theta=\pi/2,3\pi/2$. The <polar area formula> gives the <area> between radii $r_1\le r_2$ as $\frac12\int(r_2^2-r_1^2)\,d\theta$.

For $-\pi/2\le\theta\le\pi/2$, the <cardioid> lies outside the <unit circle>. The right-hand region therefore has <area>
$$
A_R=\frac12\int_{-\pi/2}^{\pi/2}\left((1+\cos\theta)^2-1\right)\,d\theta
=\int_{-\pi/2}^{\pi/2}\cos\theta\,d\theta+\frac12\int_{-\pi/2}^{\pi/2}\cos^2\theta\,d\theta
=2+\frac\pi4.
$$
On $\pi/2\le\theta\le\pi$, the <unit circle> lies outside the <cardioid>. The upper left region has <area>
$$
A_U=\frac12\int_{\pi/2}^{\pi}\left(1-(1+\cos\theta)^2\right)\,d\theta
=-\int_{\pi/2}^{\pi}\cos\theta\,d\theta-\frac12\int_{\pi/2}^{\pi}\cos^2\theta\,d\theta
=1-\frac\pi8.
$$
By <reflection symmetry> the lower left region has the same <area>. Thus
$$
\boxed{A_R=2+\frac\pi4,\qquad A_U=A_L=1-\frac\pi8.}
$$
The two left regions touch only at the <cusp>; their interiors are disconnected. As a check, the difference between the <cardioid>'s total <area> $3\pi/2$ and the <unit circle>'s <area> $\pi$ is $A_R-A_U-A_L=\pi/2$.

