= a
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}
{c}

Write $h=x-x_0$, $k=y-y_0$, and let $a=f_{xx}$, $b=f_{xy}$ and $c=f_{yy}$ be evaluated at the <critical point>. Assuming $f$ is twice continuously differentiable nearby, the <Taylor expansion> is
$$
f(x_0+h,y_0+k)-f(x_0,y_0)
=\frac12(ah^2+2bhk+ck^2)+o(h^2+k^2).
$$
The linear terms vanish because the <gradient> is zero. For $a>0$, <completing the square> gives
$$
\frac12(ah^2+2bhk+ck^2)=\frac1{2a}\left((ah+bk)^2+(ac-b^2)k^2\right).
$$
Consequently the requested constant is the <Hessian determinant>
$$
\boxed{D=f_{xx}f_{yy}-f_{xy}^2.}
$$
The displayed identity in the question is an identity for the quadratic part; for a general $f$, its right-hand side needs the remainder $o(h^2+k^2)$.

If $D>0$, the <quadratic form> is positive for every nonzero $(h,k)$. On the <unit circle> it has a positive minimum, so it dominates the remainder sufficiently near the <critical point>. Hence the point is a **strict <local minimum>**.

If $D<0$, along $k=0$ the quadratic term is $ah^2/2>0$, whereas along $h=-bk/a$ it is $Dk^2/(2a)<0$. Along each line the remainder is of smaller order. Thus values both above and below $f(x_0,y_0)$ occur arbitrarily nearby, giving a **<saddle point>**.

= b
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}
{c}

The <critical points> satisfy
$$
f_x=2x+y=0,\qquad f_y=3y^2+x=0.
$$
Substitute $x=-y/2$ to obtain $y(3y-1/2)=0$. Thus the only <critical points> are $(0,0)$ and $(-1/12,1/6)$.

The <Hessian matrix> and <Hessian determinant> are
$$
H=\begin{pmatrix}2&1\\1&6y\end{pmatrix},\qquad D=12y-1.
$$
At $(0,0)$, $D=-1$, so this is a <saddle point>. At $(-1/12,1/6)$, $D=1$ and $f_{xx}=2>0$, so this is a strict <local minimum>. Therefore
$$
\boxed{(0,0):\text{ saddle};\qquad(-1/12,1/6):\text{ strict local minimum}.}
$$
The value at the <local minimum> is $-1/432$; there is no <local maximum>.

= c
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}
{c}

Let $F=x+y+z^2$ and $g=x^2+y^2+z^2$. The <unit sphere> is <compact>, so the <continuous function> $F$ has a <global maximum> and <global minimum>. Since $\nabla g\ne0$ on the <unit sphere>, all such points satisfy the <Lagrange multiplier> equations
$$
1=2\lambda x,\qquad1=2\lambda y,\qquad2z=2\lambda z,\qquad x^2+y^2+z^2=1.
$$
Thus $x=y$ and either $z=0$ or $\lambda=1$.

If $z=0$, then $x=y=\pm1/\sqrt2$, giving $F=\pm\sqrt2$. If $\lambda=1$, then $x=y=1/2$ and $z=\pm1/\sqrt2$, giving $F=3/2$. These exhaust the candidates, so
$$
\boxed{\min F=-\sqrt2\text{ at }(-1/\sqrt2,-1/\sqrt2,0),\qquad
\max F=\frac32\text{ at }(1/2,1/2,\pm1/\sqrt2).}
$$

