= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

Put $q=1-p$. Since $X$ takes positive integer values, $\mathbb P(X>0)=1=q^0$. Taking differences of the given tail <probabilities> therefore yields, also for $k=1$,
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(X=k)=\mathbb P(X>k-1)-\mathbb P(X>k)=pq^{k-1},\qquad k\ge1.}
$$
This is the <probability mass function> of the <geometric distribution> on $\{1,2,\ldots\}$.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

Using the <geometric series>, the <probability generating function> is
$$
G_X(s)=\mathbb E(s^X)=\sum_{k\ge1}pq^{k-1}s^k
=\boxed{\frac{ps}{1-qs}},\qquad |qs|<1.
$$
Differentiation gives
$$
\boxed{G'_X(s)=\frac{p(1-qs)+pqs}{(1-qs)^2}=\frac{p}{(1-s+ps)^2}.}
$$

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}
{c}

The <mean> is the <derivative> at one of the <probability generating function>. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\mathbb E X=G'_X(1)=\frac{p}{p^2}=\frac1p.}
$$
Evaluation at one is valid here because the <probability generating function> has radius of convergence $1/(1-p)>1$.

= iv
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}
{c}

The event $W>k$ occurs exactly when both $X_1>k$ and $X_2>k$. By <independence>,
$$
\mathbb P(W>k)=q^kq^k=q^{2k}=\left(1-(2p-p^2)\right)^k.
$$
Thus
$$
\boxed{W\sim\operatorname{Geom}(2p-p^2).}
$$
The new success <probability> $2p-p^2$ is the chance that at least one of the two trials succeeds.

= b
{parent=4}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

Since $Y\ge1$, its finite <mean> satisfies $1\le\mu<\infty$. Every $q_k=kp_k/\mu$ is nonnegative, and
$$
\sum_{k\ge1}q_k=\frac1\mu\sum_{k\ge1}kp_k=\frac{\mathbb E Y}{\mu}=1.
$$
Thus $(q_k)$ is a **<probability mass function>**. It defines the <size-biased distribution> of $Y$: an outcome of size $k$ receives $k$ times its original relative weight.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

By the definition of a <probability generating function>,
$$
G_{\widetilde Y}(s)=\sum_{k\ge1}\frac{kp_k}{\mu}s^k
=\boxed{\frac{s}{\mu}G'_Y(s)}.
$$
Termwise differentiation of the <power series> is valid for $|s|<1$. At $s=1$, the equality extends by the finite <mean> assumption, and both sides equal one.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}
{c}

For the <geometric distribution>, $\mu=1/p$, so the <probability generating function> of the <size-biased distribution> is
$$
G_{\widetilde Y}(s)=\frac{p^2s}{(1-qs)^2}.
$$
If $X_1,X_2$ are <independent and identically distributed> with <geometric distribution> of parameter $p$, the <probability generating function of an independent sum> gives
$$
G_{X_1+X_2-1}(s)=s^{-1}\left(\frac{ps}{1-qs}\right)^2
=\frac{p^2s}{(1-qs)^2}.
$$
At zero, interpret the expression by its continuous extension. The <probability generating functions> agree, hence
$$
\boxed{\widetilde Y\overset d=X_1+X_2-1,\qquad X_1,X_2\sim\operatorname{Geom}(p)\text{ independently}.}
$$
One can also check the <probability mass functions> directly: for $k\ge1$, there are $k$ positive integer pairs with sum $k+1$, each having <probability> $p^2q^{k-1}$. Thus both sides assign <probability> $kp^2q^{k-1}$ to $k$.

= 5
{parent=Mathematics III}
{scope}

= a
{parent=5}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

A <partition of a set> $\Omega$ is a collection of nonempty <subsets> whose union is $\Omega$ and which are pairwise disjoint:
$$
\bigcup_{i\in I}B_i=\Omega,\qquad B_i\cap B_j=\varnothing\quad(i\ne j).
$$
For use with <probabilities>, the $B_i$ must also be <events> in the <sample space>.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

For a finite or countable <partition of a set> into <events> $B_i$, the <law of total probability> states
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(A)=\sum_{i:\,\mathbb P(B_i)>0}\mathbb P(A\mid B_i)\mathbb P(B_i).}
$$
The <events> $A\cap B_i$ are pairwise disjoint and have union $A$, so countable additivity proves the formula. Terms with $\mathbb P(B_i)=0$ contribute zero and are omitted to avoid undefined <conditional probabilities>.

= b
{parent=5}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

There are two paths from $X$ back to $X$ in two steps: $X\to X\to X$ and $X\to Y\to X$. By the <markov property>, their <probabilities> multiply along each path. Therefore
$$
\boxed{p_2=\frac12\cdot\frac12+\frac12\cdot\frac38=\frac14+\frac3{16}=\frac7{16}.}
$$

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

Let $q_n=\mathbb P(M_n=Y)$. The <law of total probability> applied to the current <markov chain state> gives
$$
p_n=\frac12p_{n-1}+\frac38q_{n-1},\qquad q_{n-1}=\frac12p_{n-2}\quad(n\ge2).
$$
The second equality holds because $Y$ can only be reached from $X$. Eliminating $q_{n-1}$ gives the <linear recurrence relation>
$$
\boxed{16p_n=8p_{n-1}+3p_{n-2},\qquad n\ge2.}
$$

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}
{c}

The <characteristic equation of a linear recurrence> is
$$
16r^2-8r-3=(4r-3)(4r+1)=0.
$$
Its distinct roots are $3/4$ and $-1/4$, so $p_n=A(3/4)^n+B(-1/4)^n$. The <initial conditions> are $p_0=1$ and $p_1=1/2$, giving $A+B=1$ and $3A-B=2$. Hence $A=3/4$, $B=1/4$, and
$$
\boxed{p_n=\frac34\left(\frac34\right)^n+\frac14\left(-\frac14\right)^n
=\frac{3^{n+1}+(-1)^n}{4^{n+1}},\qquad n\ge0.}
$$

= iv
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}
{c}

Starting from $X$ at time $n$, the <markov property> and the time-independent transition rules give $\mathbb P(M_{2n}=X\mid M_n=X)=p_n$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(M_n=X\mid M_{2n}=X)=\frac{\mathbb P(M_n=X,M_{2n}=X)}{p_{2n}}=\frac{p_n^2}{p_{2n}}.}
$$
Write $p_n=\frac34(\frac34)^n\left(1+\frac13(-\frac13)^n\right)$. The <conditional probability> becomes
$$
\frac34\frac{\left(1+\frac13(-\frac13)^n\right)^2}{1+\frac13(\frac19)^n}
\longrightarrow\boxed{\frac34}.
$$
The conditioning selects paths which have avoided the <absorbing state> up to time $2n$; it is therefore quite different from the unconditioned <probability> $p_n$, which tends to zero.

= 6
{parent=Mathematics III}
{scope}

= a
{parent=6}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

For the rate-one <exponential distribution>, the <probability density function> is $e^{-t}$ for $t\ge0$. By <integration by parts>,
$$
\mathbb E T=\int_0^\infty te^{-t}\,dt=1,\qquad
\mathbb E(T^2)=\int_0^\infty t^2e^{-t}\,dt=2.
$$
Thus
$$
\boxed{\mathbb E T=1,\qquad\operatorname{Var}(T)=2-1^2=1.}
$$

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

By <independence>, the <expected value> of the product factors:
$$
\boxed{\mathbb E(TU)=\mathbb E T\,\mathbb E U=1.}
$$

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}
{c}

The products $TU$ and $UV$ share $U$, so they are not <independent>. Using the definition of <covariance> and the <independence> of $T,U,V$,
$$
\operatorname{Cov}(TU,UV)=\mathbb E(TU^2V)-\mathbb E(TU)\mathbb E(UV)
=(\mathbb E T)(\mathbb E U^2)(\mathbb E V)-1=2-1=1.
$$
By contrast, $TU$ and $VW$ depend on disjoint sets of <independent random variables>, so they are <independent> and have zero <covariance>. Hence
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{Cov}(TU,UV)=1,\qquad\operatorname{Cov}(TU,VW)=0.}
$$

= b
{parent=6}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

Assume finite second moments and write $Z_i=X_i-\mathbb E X_i$. Expanding the square in the definition of <variance> gives
$$
\operatorname{Var}(X_1+X_2)=\mathbb E[(Z_1+Z_2)^2]
=\mathbb E Z_1^2+\mathbb E Z_2^2+2\mathbb E(Z_1Z_2).
$$
Recognizing the two <variances> and the <covariance>, we obtain
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{Var}(X_1+X_2)=\operatorname{Var}(X_1)+\operatorname{Var}(X_2)+2\operatorname{Cov}(X_1,X_2).}
$$

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

For <random variables> with finite second moments, the <variance of a sum> is
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{Var}\left(\sum_{i=1}^nX_i\right)=\sum_{i=1}^n\operatorname{Var}(X_i)+2\sum_{1\le i<j\le n}\operatorname{Cov}(X_i,X_j).}
$$
No <independence> assumption is needed.

= c
{parent=6}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

Write $A_i=Y_{i-1}Y_i$, so $S_n=\sum_{i=1}^nA_i$. Each product has <mean> one, and its <variance> is
$$
\operatorname{Var}(A_i)=\mathbb E(Y_{i-1}^2)\mathbb E(Y_i^2)-1=2\cdot2-1=3.
$$
Adjacent products share one factor, giving <covariance> one by part (a)(iii). Products whose indices differ by at least two involve disjoint <independent random variables>, so their <covariance> is zero. There are $n-1$ adjacent pairs. By the <variance of a sum>,
$$
\boxed{\mathbb E S_n=n,\qquad\operatorname{Var}(S_n)=3n+2(n-1)=5n-2,\qquad n\ge1.}
$$

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

By <Chebyshev's inequality> and part (i),
$$
0\le\mathbb P\left(\frac{S_n}{n}>1+\epsilon\right)
\le\mathbb P\left(\left|\frac{S_n}{n}-1\right|\ge\epsilon\right)
\le\frac{\operatorname{Var}(S_n)}{n^2\epsilon^2}
=\frac{5n-2}{n^2\epsilon^2}\longrightarrow0.
$$
This actually proves the stronger statement $\boxed{S_n/n\xrightarrow{\mathbb P}1}$, namely <convergence in probability>, despite the <statistical dependence> between adjacent products.

= 7
{parent=Mathematics III}
{scope}

= a
{parent=7}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

Let $M=\max_iX_i$. For an observed sample in the nonnegative orthant, the <likelihood function> under the <uniform distribution> is
$$
L(\theta)=\theta^{-n}\mathbf1_{\{\theta\ge M\}},\qquad\theta>0.
$$
It is zero for $\theta<M$, and decreases with $\theta$ on $[M,\infty)$. Thus the <maximum likelihood estimation> problem is solved at the smallest admissible value:
$$
\boxed{\widehat\theta=M=\max_{1\le i\le n}X_i.}
$$
Under the model $M>0$ with <probability> one. The all-zero sample is a null event on which the <likelihood function> has no finite maximizer in the parameter space $\theta>0$.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

For $0\le t\le1$, <independence> and the <uniform distribution> give
$$
\mathbb P\left(\frac M\theta\le t\right)=\mathbb P(X_1\le\theta t,\ldots,X_n\le\theta t)=t^n.
$$
Therefore the <cumulative distribution function> is
$$
\boxed{F_{M/\theta}(t)=\begin{cases}0,&t<0,\\t^n,&0\le t\le1,\\1,&t>1.\end{cases}}
$$
It contains no $\theta$, so $M/\theta$ is a <pivotal quantity>.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}
{c}

Differentiating the <cumulative distribution function> in part (ii) gives the <probability density function> $nt^{n-1}$ on $(0,1)$. Hence
$$
\mathbb E\left(\frac M\theta\right)=\int_0^1t\,nt^{n-1}\,dt=\frac n{n+1},\qquad
\mathbb E M=\frac n{n+1}\theta.
$$
Thus an <unbiased estimator> based on the sample maximum is
$$
\boxed{\widetilde\theta=\frac{n+1}{n}\widehat\theta.}
$$

= iv
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}
{c}

Each observation from the <uniform distribution> has <mean> $\theta/2$. By <linearity of expectation>, $\mathbb E\sum_iX_i=n\theta/2$, so another <unbiased estimator> is
$$
\boxed{\theta^*=\frac2n\sum_{i=1}^nX_i=2\overline X.}
$$

= v
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=v}
{c}

The <pivotal quantity> $U=M/\theta$ lies in $[0,1]$ and satisfies $\mathbb P(U\le t)=t^n$. Thus
$$
\mathbb P\left(\alpha^{1/n}\le\frac M\theta\le1\right)=1-\alpha.
$$
Rearranging these inequalities yields the exact <confidence interval>
$$
\boxed{\left[\widehat\theta,\frac{\widehat\theta}{\alpha^{1/n}}\right].}
$$
For every $\theta>0$, its coverage <probability> is exactly $1-\alpha$; the result uses the finite-sample <probability distribution>, with no <normal approximation>.

= b
{parent=7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}
{c}

The <log-likelihood function> for the <Laplace distribution> is
$$
\ell(\kappa)=-n\log2-\sum_{i=1}^n|Y_i-\kappa|.
$$
Thus <maximum likelihood estimation> is equivalent to minimizing the sum of <absolute values>. Write $n=2m+1$ and sort the data as $Y_{(1)}\le\cdots\le Y_{(n)}$. Between observations, if $r$ values lie to the left of $\kappa$, the <derivative> of $\sum_i|Y_i-\kappa|$ is $r-(n-r)=2r-n$. It is negative before the middle observation and positive after it. The minimum occurs at the <sample median>:
$$
\boxed{\widehat\kappa=Y_{(m+1)}=Y_{((n+1)/2)}.}
$$
This minimizer remains unique for odd $n$ even when some observations coincide, since fewer than half the observations can lie strictly on either side of the middle value.

= 8
{parent=Mathematics III}
{scope}

= a
{parent=8}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

Treat the explanatory variables as fixed and put $S_{xx}=\sum_i x_i^2$, $S_{zz}=\sum_i z_i^2$. To identify both slopes, assume $S_{xx}>0$ and $S_{zz}>0$. The given centring and <orthogonality> conditions make the three columns $1,x,z$ pairwise <orthogonal>.

The <log-likelihood function> in the <normal linear model>, up to a constant independent of the coefficients, is
$$
\ell(\beta_0,\beta_1,\beta_2)=-\frac1{2\sigma^2}\sum_i(Y_i-\beta_0-\beta_1x_i-\beta_2z_i)^2.
$$
Thus <maximum likelihood estimation> minimizes the <residual sum of squares>. Setting its <partial derivatives> to zero gives
$$
\sum_i(Y_i-\beta_0-\beta_1x_i-\beta_2z_i)=0,\quad
\sum_i x_i(Y_i-\beta_0-\beta_1x_i-\beta_2z_i)=0,\quad
\sum_i z_i(Y_i-\beta_0-\beta_1x_i-\beta_2z_i)=0.
$$
Using $\sum x_i=\sum z_i=\sum x_iz_i=0$ decouples these equations:
$$
\boxed{\widehat\beta_0=\overline Y,\qquad
\widehat\beta_1=\frac{\sum_i x_iY_i}{S_{xx}},\qquad
\widehat\beta_2=\frac{\sum_i z_iY_i}{S_{zz}}.}
$$
The <Hessian matrix> of the <residual sum of squares> is $2\operatorname{diag}(n,S_{xx},S_{zz})$, which is positive definite. These are therefore the unique minimizers. If either squared sum is zero, the associated explanatory variable vanishes identically and its coefficient cannot be identified.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

Substitute the <linear regression model> into the estimators and use the centring and <orthogonality> conditions:
$$
\widehat\beta_0=\beta_0+\frac1n\sum_i\epsilon_i,\qquad
\widehat\beta_1=\beta_1+\frac{\sum_i x_i\epsilon_i}{S_{xx}}.
$$
A linear combination of <independent> <normal random variables> is normal. Their <means> are $\beta_0$ and $\beta_1$, while <variance additivity for independent random variables> gives
$$
\operatorname{Var}(\widehat\beta_0)=\frac{n\sigma^2}{n^2}=\frac{\sigma^2}{n},\qquad
\operatorname{Var}(\widehat\beta_1)=\frac{\sigma^2\sum_i x_i^2}{S_{xx}^2}=\frac{\sigma^2}{S_{xx}}.
$$
Consequently
$$
\boxed{\widehat\beta_0\sim N(\beta_0,\sigma^2/n),\qquad
\widehat\beta_1\sim N(\beta_1,\sigma^2/S_{xx}).}
$$

= b
{parent=8}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

The <central limit theorem> states that if $W_1,W_2,\ldots$ are <independent and identically distributed> with finite <mean> $\mu$ and <variance> $0<\tau^2<\infty$, then
$$
\boxed{\frac{\sum_{i=1}^nW_i-n\mu}{\tau\sqrt n}\xrightarrow{d}N(0,1).}
$$
Here $\xrightarrow d$ denotes <convergence in distribution>.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

The algebraic identity $\widehat\beta_0-\beta_0=n^{-1}\sum_i\epsilon_i$ does not require a <normal distribution> for the errors. Applying the <central limit theorem> to their <sample mean> gives
$$
\frac{\sqrt n(\widehat\beta_0-\beta_0)}{\sigma}\xrightarrow d N(0,1).
$$
Thus, for large $n$,
$$
\boxed{\widehat\beta_0\ \text{is approximately }N(\beta_0,\sigma^2/n).}
$$
Its <mean> $\beta_0$ and <variance> $\sigma^2/n$ remain exact, even though its <normal distribution> is now only an approximation. With the error <probability distribution> unspecified, the formula is retained as an estimator; it is not being asserted to remain a <maximum likelihood estimation> formula.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}
{c}

**No, not from the stated assumptions alone.** The centered slope is a weighted sum,
$$
\widehat\beta_1-\beta_1=\frac{\sum_i x_i\epsilon_i}{S_{xx}},
$$
and the weighted errors need not be identically distributed. The ordinary <central limit theorem> therefore does not apply directly.

For a concrete counterexample, for every $n\ge3$ choose
$$
x=(1,-1,0,\ldots,0),\qquad z=(1,1,-2,0,\ldots,0).
$$
These satisfy all three centring and <orthogonality> assumptions, and both explanatory columns are nonzero. Let the errors be <independent> with <Rademacher distribution>, taking values $\pm1$ with equal <probability>. Then
$$
\widehat\beta_1-\beta_1=\frac{\epsilon_1-\epsilon_2}{2},
$$
which takes values $-1,0,1$ with <probabilities> $1/4,1/2,1/4$ for every $n$. Increasing the sample size adds no information about this slope, and the standardized estimator does not approach a <normal distribution>.

A useful additional assumption for <asymptotic normality of a weighted regression slope> is that no single design value dominates:
$$
\frac{\max_i x_i^2}{\sum_i x_i^2}\longrightarrow0.
$$
Under this condition and the given finite error <variance>, the <Lindeberg-Feller central limit theorem> yields
$$
\frac{\sqrt{S_{xx}}(\widehat\beta_1-\beta_1)}{\sigma}\xrightarrow d N(0,1),
$$
justifying the approximation $N(\beta_1,\sigma^2/S_{xx})$ for the slope as well.

= 9
{parent=Mathematics III}
{scope}

= a
{parent=9}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}
{c}

Use the corrected decomposition
$$
y_{ij}-w_j=(y_{ij}-\mu_j)+(\mu_j-w_j).
$$
The hint's minus sign before $(\mu_j-w_j)$ is a typographical error. Squaring, summing, and using $\sum_i(y_{ij}-\mu_j)=0$ for each coordinate gives the <centroid sum-of-squares identity>
$$
\sum_{i,j}(y_{ij}-w_j)^2
=\sum_{i,j}(y_{ij}-\mu_j)^2
+2\sum_j(\mu_j-w_j)\underbrace{\sum_i(y_{ij}-\mu_j)}_{0}
+m\sum_j(\mu_j-w_j)^2.
$$
Since $m>0$ and $w\ne\mu$, the final term is strictly positive. Hence
$$
\boxed{\sum_{i,j}(y_{ij}-\mu_j)^2<\sum_{i,j}(y_{ij}-w_j)^2.}
$$
Thus the <centroid> uniquely minimizes the sum of squared <Euclidean distances> to the observations.

= b
{parent=9}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}
{c}

Write $x_i\in\mathbb R^p$ for row $i$ of the <data matrix>. The <k-means clustering> objective is the within-cluster sum of squared <Euclidean distances>:
$$
\boxed{J(C_1,\ldots,C_K;m_1,\ldots,m_K)=\sum_{k=1}^K\sum_{i\in C_k}\|x_i-m_k\|^2.}
$$
The nonempty $C_k$ form a <partition of a set> of observation indices. For fixed clusters, part (a) shows that the best $m_k$ is their <centroid>.

Initialize distinct centres, for example by choosing $K\le n$ distinct observations. Then repeat the following steps:

* Assign each observation to a nearest centre in <Euclidean distance>. Keep its current assignment on an exact tie; for a previously unassigned observation use a fixed ordering to resolve ties.
* If a cluster is empty, choose an observation from a cluster with at least two members and make it the empty cluster's sole member, placing its centre at that observation. A definite rule is to choose an eligible observation farthest from its currently assigned centre, breaking ties by observation index. Repeat until all clusters are nonempty.
* Replace each centre by the <arithmetic mean> of its assigned observations.
* After updating, check whether every observation is still assigned to a nearest updated centre, retaining its assignment on ties. If so, stop; otherwise repeat.

The empty-cluster repair is possible for $K\le n$ and cannot increase $J$: the transferred observation now has squared distance zero, while all other squared distances stay unchanged before the centre update.

Assignment cannot increase $J$, and any changed assignment strictly decreases it under the tie rule. Updating centres cannot increase $J$ by the <centroid sum-of-squares identity>. After an update, the centres are determined by the <partition of a set>. There are only finitely many possible assignments. Unless this is the initial round, any nonterminal round starts with an assignment that strictly reduces $J$; repairing empty clusters and updating cannot undo that decrease. Thus a partition cannot recur, proving <finite termination of k-means clustering>. The final result need not be a <global minimum>; different initial centres can lead to different final clusterings.

= c
{parent=9}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}
{c}

Sort the distinct observations as $x_{(1)}<\cdots<x_{(n)}$. For ordered centres $m_1<\cdots<m_r$, a point is closer to $m_j$ than to $m_{j+1}$ precisely when it lies to the left of their midpoint. Thus each <Voronoi cell> is an <interval>, with either choice allowed for an endpoint tie. Consequently, after assignment, each nonempty cluster is a contiguous block of the sorted observations.

The <arithmetic means> of disjoint ordered blocks are strictly ordered, so updating centres preserves this order. The empty-cluster repair in part (b) preserves contiguous blocks too: an observation farthest from its assigned centre lies at an endpoint of its block, and removing that endpoint makes a new singleton block. In one dimension with distinct observations, choosing $K$ distinct observations as initial centres makes the first assignment nonempty.

The <one-dimensional k-means iteration bound> now follows by counting block partitions. Choosing the $K-1$ gaps between consecutive nonempty blocks gives
$$
\binom{n-1}{K-1}\le n^{K-1}
$$
possibilities. By the strict-decrease argument in part (b), no such partition can recur before <algorithm termination>. Counting a step as an assignment, any empty-cluster repairs, and a centre update, with the stability check performed after that update, gives
$$
\boxed{\text{number of steps}\le\binom{n-1}{K-1}\le n^{K-1}.}
$$
For $K=1$, one update to the overall <arithmetic mean> suffices. Arbitrary changes between tied centres would invalidate the strict-decrease argument, which is why the stable tie convention in part (b) matters.
